  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{6n}}{{9}^{kn}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{3}^{6}}{{9}^{k}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{9}^{3}}{{9}^{k}})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{({9}^{3-k})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r={9}^{3-k}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{9}^{3-k}| & <1 \\\\ {9}^{3-k} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because ${9}^{3-k}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${9}^{3-k}=1$ has the solution $k=3.$ Now, since ${9}^{3-k}$ decreases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${9}^{3-k}<1$ must be $k>3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
